  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through Sunday)\nIssued at 1016 PM CDT Sun Sep 27 2026\n\nThursday will bring one more hotter than normal day before a\npotentially wetter pattern develops into the weekend. An upper ridge\nwill be holding tight over the southeast U.S. Thursday into Friday as\na sluggish upper low sits over the southern High Plains. Complicating\nthis pattern will be a TUTT over the central Gulf of America that\nwill be lifted northward on Saturday into Sunday across the Gulf\nStates. This will produce a stout southerly tropical flow into the\nmid South and TN Valley, yielding increasing rain and thunderstorms\nover a wide area. Another feature that will help this regime will be\na wedge front that will be dropping southwest through the Carolinas\ninto GA and perhaps far northeast AL, providing another isentropic\nlifting source. The upper low finally becomes an open wave on Sunday\nlifting into the OH Valley and absorbing the TUTT into the flow\nacross the TN Valley and eventually OH Valley. Thus low to medium\nchances of rain and thunderstorms exist starting Thursday and Friday,\nand increasing Saturday and Sunday. With the tropical flow,\ntemperatures will be held in the upper 70s to lower 80s by the\nweekend, with dew points in the upper 60s to lower 70s. It is too\nearly to specify the excessive rainfall risk, but it will certainly\nbe monitored through the week.",
